#{[17592186079516 #edb/ref 1061 4833 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186079516 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "0a4e1771-2f14-58d7-9a7d-62eb186154dd" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186079516 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186078568 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186079690 #edb/ref 1064 "Consider the function $y=f(x)$ below. Suppose we know that $f(2)=5$ and $f'(2)=1.$ Based on this information, how can we approximate the value of $f(2.75)?$\n\n![](images/25/25baabc5d4193a480d09370faa104d34edc14b2aa09759f5976d592a387fb398.png)\n\nFirst, recall that the tangent line to $y=f(x)$ at $x=a$ is given by\n\n$$\ny-f(a) = f'(a)(x-a).\n$$\n\nSolving for $y$ in the above, we get \n\n$$\ny= f(a)+ f'(a)(x-a)\n$$\n\nNow, provided that $x$ is close to $a,$ we can assume that $y\\approx f(x) ,$ which gives us the approximation\n\n$$\nf(x)\\approx f(a)+ f'(a)(x-a).\n$$\n\nWe often represent the tangent line approximation using the notation $L(x),$ as follows:\n\n$$\nL(x)= f(a)+ f'(a)(x-a)\n$$\n\nIn this case, we want the tangent line approximation at $a=2.$ Substituting this value along with the given information, we find\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L(x) & =f(a)+{f}^{′}(a)(x-a) \\\\  & =f(2)+{f}^{′}(2)(x-2) \\\\  & =(5)+(1)(x-2) \\\\  & =x+3.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, let's take a look at the graphs. Notice that in the neighborhood of $x=2$, the function and its tangent line approximation are very close. The line $L(x)$ is a good approximation of the function for points close to $x=2.$\n\n![](images/c1/c1de31c316c019e8faf466f64a0e6ff24a29935a7ec354c2acdf18d3069b8d47.png)\n\nAs we can see, ${\\color{blue}L(2.75)}$ and ${\\color{red}f(2.75)}$ are quite close. So, we can approximate $f(2.75)$ using $L(2.75)\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(2.75) & ≈L(2.75) \\\\  & =(2.75)+3 \\\\  & =5.75.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe tangent line approximation\n\n$$\nL(x)= f(a)+ f'(a)(x-a)\n$$\n\nis sometimes also called the **linear approximation** of the function $f(x)$ at the point $x=a.$ The process of approximating the function by its tangent is called **linearization.**" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186079690 #edb/ref 1065 579 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186079690 #edb/ref 1066 #uuid "1fd16cbf-e232-56c9-84ba-d6b6ff2d147a" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186079690 #edb/ref 1067 "Introduction" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186079691 #edb/ref 1064 "Let $L(x)$ be the tangent line approximation of $y = f(x)$ at $x=a,$ as shown in the pictures below. \n\n| ![](images/ad/ad7375c544eed881d96d07b449d9003c7df813bdddcdaf29e82bfda6bd4cb283.png) | ![](images/b5/b5b3cd71da3243a5848021831403adde07f1487ff1077f3359d2564ba0f4b686.png) |\n| --- | --- |\n\nWe can make the following conclusions:\n\n- When the function is *concave up*, the tangent line is *below* the graph (see the picture on the left). Then, \n\n$$\nL(x) \\lt f(x)\n$$\n\nnear $x=a,$ and we say that $L(x)$ gives an *underestimate* of the function.\n\n- When the function is *concave down*, the tangent line is *above* the graph (see the picture on the right). Then, \n\n$$\nL(x) \\gt f(x)\n$$\n\nnear $x=a,$ and we say that $L(x)$ gives an *overestimate* of the function." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186079691 #edb/ref 1065 582 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186079691 #edb/ref 1066 #uuid "717ddca8-0e12-5d53-9384-635de0a676ce" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186079691 #edb/ref 1067 "Underestimate and Overestimate Approximations" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186119926 #edb/ref 1037 #edb/ref 17592186048499 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186119926 #edb/ref 1037 #edb/ref 17592186049716 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186119926 #edb/ref 1037 #edb/ref 17592186050367 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186119926 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186120145 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186119926 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186120150 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186119926 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186275357 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186119926 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186275358 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186119926 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186275359 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186119926 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186275361 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186119926 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186275389 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186119926 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186417219 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186119926 #edb/ref 1039 "Determining Whether a Line Is a Subspace of Two-Dimensional Space" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186119926 #edb/ref 1041 #edb/ref 17592186119500 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186119926 #edb/ref 1042 #uuid "19c33c51-bf11-5a81-a0fd-8381d8e0b9d7" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186119927 #edb/ref 1037 #edb/ref 17592186048850 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186119927 #edb/ref 1037 #edb/ref 17592186049485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186119927 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186120146 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186119927 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186120151 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186119927 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186275363 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186119927 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186275390 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186119927 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186275391 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186119927 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186275392 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186119927 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186275393 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186119927 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186275394 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186119927 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186376054 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186119927 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186425728 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186119927 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186489255 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186119927 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186492036 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186119927 #edb/ref 1039 "Determining Whether a Line Is a Subspace of Three-Dimensional Space" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186119927 #edb/ref 1041 #edb/ref 17592186119501 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186119927 #edb/ref 1042 #uuid "83796fe8-f045-5dbc-94d1-6d6e031f3ed8" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186119928 #edb/ref 1037 #edb/ref 17592186050830 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186119928 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186120152 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186119928 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186120153 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186119928 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186275364 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186119928 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186275395 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186119928 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186275396 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186119928 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186275397 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186119928 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186275398 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186119928 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186275399 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186119928 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186376047 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186119928 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186376061 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186119928 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186376068 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186119928 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186489173 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186119928 #edb/ref 1039 "Determining Whether a Plane Is a Subspace of Three-Dimensional Space" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186119928 #edb/ref 1041 #edb/ref 17592186119502 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186119928 #edb/ref 1042 #uuid "4f3018fd-9d16-578f-b050-966890844c9b" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186120047 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186120884 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186120047 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186120885 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186120047 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186120886 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186120047 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186120887 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186120047 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186120888 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186120047 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186120889 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186120047 #edb/ref 1044 #edb/ref 17592186045463 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186120047 #edb/ref 1045 "Subspaces of N-Dimensional Space" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186120047 #edb/ref 1046 #edb/ref 17592186120884 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186120047 #edb/ref 1049 #edb/ref 17592186051046 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186120047 #edb/ref 1050 660.0 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186120047 #edb/ref 1053 #uuid "dcdc8ecc-c5d8-56ba-b664-0870beb832b2" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186120884 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186120885 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186120884 #edb/ref 1061 41645 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186120884 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "7f009ee8-4a5e-5da7-b539-b2991cbd9d04" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186120884 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186121126 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186120885 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186120888 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186120885 #edb/ref 1061 41646 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186120885 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "a7eb434d-3210-5f2f-b5ec-18781d0fa02d" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186120885 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186119926 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186120886 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186120889 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186120886 #edb/ref 1061 41660 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186120886 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "911d42c3-5fd0-515e-abcd-cfaab6aa6926" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186120886 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186119927 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186120887 #edb/ref 1061 41667 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186120887 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "8b4b4898-6c15-52d9-96fe-d57251b08369" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186120887 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186119928 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186120888 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186120886 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186120888 #edb/ref 1061 41674 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186120888 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "124c571f-28f0-56fa-a68d-76ef241f4618" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186120888 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186121127 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186120889 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186120887 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186120889 #edb/ref 1061 41675 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186120889 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "2d8c8e20-696b-5df3-beaf-a78962ce0420" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186120889 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186121128 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186121126 #edb/ref 1064 "A **subspace** of $\\mathbb{R}^n$ is a subset of $\\mathbb R^n$ that is closed  under vector addition and scalar multiplication. In other words: \n\n$H\\subseteq \\mathbb R^n$ is a subspace of $\\mathbb{R}^n$ if and only if it satisfies the following properties:\n\n- *Closure under vector addition:* \n\n$\\qquad$ If $\\mathbf{u}$ and $\\mathbf{v}$ are in $H,$ then their sum $\\mathbf{u}+\\mathbf{v}$ is also in $H.$\n\n- *Closure under scalar multiplication:* \n\n$\\qquad$ If $\\mathbf{u}$ is in $H,$ then $c\\cdot \\mathbf{u}$ is also in $H$ for any scalar number $c.$\n\nFor example, consider the line $L$ with equation $y=3x$ in $\\mathbb{R}^2.$ Is $L$ a subspace of $\\mathbb{R}^2?$ \n\nFirst, notice that we can express the points on $L$ as the following set:\n\n![](images/1f/1f380f9928da2dd44ff157a72fce97c02ae44626df515d52ece6ff5feee302d2.svg)\n\nIn other words, $L$ contains all vectors of the form ![](images/90/90996c2ffae29c3bd9cd3ab398cca4dd7e1b8fbe275de8520a72a16e504fb0f3.svg) where $x$ is a real number.\n\nTo determine whether $L$ is a subspace of $\\mathbb R^2,$ we need to check the two conditions above. \n\n- Let's pick two arbitrary vectors in $L,$ namely, ![](images/59/59be22e80d0be32c84a8c2bdb2f2c96b23a0941d4aba26bdefa078fd299c137c.svg) and ![](images/d7/d78ea4fcfbd469e88b7fe46e28ac6f396a538c577f4defdd1b3d60e60df3c4b7.svg) If we compute their sum, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u+v & =[\\begin{aligned}{u}_{1} \\\\ 3{u}_{1}\\end{aligned}]+[\\begin{aligned}{v}_{1} \\\\ 3{v}_{1}\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}{u}_{1}+{v}_{1} \\\\ 3{u}_{1}+3{v}_{1}\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}{u}_{1}+{v}_{1} \\\\ 3\\cdot ({u}_{1}+{v}_{1})\\end{aligned}],\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich satisfies the condition $y=3x.$ Therefore, $\\mathbf{u}+\\mathbf{v} \\in L. \\quad {\\color{green}\\checkmark}$\n\n- Let's pick an arbitrary vector in $L,$ namely, ![](images/75/759c7883ad2772f20a090ad2f8c5dfe01031ca31e2b5f0e7681c945c9256c91d.svg) Then, for any scalar $c$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}c\\cdot u & =c\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}{u}_{1} \\\\ 3{u}_{1}\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}c{u}_{1} \\\\ 3\\cdot c{u}_{1}\\end{aligned}],\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich satisfies the condition $y=3x.$ Therefore, $c\\cdot \\mathbf{u}\\in L. \\quad {\\color{green}\\checkmark}$\n\nThe set $L$ satisfies both conditions. So, we can conclude that it is a subspace of $\\mathbb{R}^2.$\n\n**Note:** Since the second condition must be satisfied for any $c,$ including $c=0,$ we can conclude that every subspace must contain the zero vector $\\mathbf{0}.$ So, if a set does not contain $\\mathbf{0}$ then it is *not* a subspace of $\\mathbb{R}^n.$\n\nFor instance, the set \n\n![](images/a1/a15e0b4fc4544421e572d15b8fd016c3fa8721e0ecf3f8808a469c3252d1f6d6.svg)\n\ndoes *not* contain the zero vector (since $0 \\neq 0 + 1$), and consequently it is *not* a subspace of $\\mathbb{R}^2.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186121126 #edb/ref 1065 6853 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186121126 #edb/ref 1066 #uuid "ee2c580c-5386-5e06-9b58-e9e1dd398cd3" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186121126 #edb/ref 1067 "Introduction" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186121127 #edb/ref 1064 "Recall that $H$ is a subspace of $\\mathbb{R}^n$ if and only if it satisfies the following properties:\n\n*Closure under vector addition:* \n\n $\\qquad$ If $\\mathbf{u}$ and $\\mathbf{v}$ are in $H,$ then their sum $\\mathbf{u}+\\mathbf{v}$ is also in $H.$\n\n*Closure under scalar multiplication:* \n\n $\\qquad$ If $\\mathbf{u}$ is in $H,$ then $c\\cdot \\mathbf{u}$ is also in $H$ for any scalar number $c.$\n\nLet $L$ be the line in $\\mathbb{R}^3$ that passes through the points $(0,0,0)$ and $(4,1,-2).$ Is $L$ a subspace of $\\mathbb{R}^3?$ \n\nFirst, note that the direction vector of the line $L$ is \n![](images/25/25fa34994b33e0d11fd8990dddbfda276857e699018b4701c6e2eaf13acb0c20.svg)\nThis means that the vector equation of the line is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r & =[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]+t[\\begin{aligned}4 \\\\ 1 \\\\ -2\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}4t \\\\ t \\\\ -2t\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nfor $t\\in\\mathbb R,$ and the corresponding Cartesian equation is \n\n$$\n\\dfrac x4=y=\\dfrac z{-2}.\n$$\n\nTo determine whether $L$ is a subspace, we need to check the two conditions above:\n\n- Let's pick two arbitrary vectors in $L,$ namely, ![](images/d7/d7f1a3cf1625ff12bc96324dc428e1c95d271d8a72de603b671f722b9173281d.svg) and ![](images/25/255fc61225b017a51ae60b1d157787ac4b60fe596ebaa8296b48053b87b2eae7.svg) If we compute their sum, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u+v & =[\\begin{aligned}4{u}_{1} \\\\ {u}_{1} \\\\ -2{u}_{1}\\end{aligned}]+[\\begin{aligned}4{v}_{1} \\\\ {v}_{1} \\\\ -2{v}_{1}\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}4{u}_{1}+4{v}_{1} \\\\ {u}_{1}+{v}_{1} \\\\ (-2{u}_{1})+(-2{v}_{1})\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}4\\cdot ({u}_{1}+{v}_{1}) \\\\ {u}_{1}+{v}_{1} \\\\ (-2)\\cdot ({u}_{1}+{v}_{1})\\end{aligned}],\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich satisfies the condition $\\dfrac x4=y=\\dfrac z{-2}.$ Therefore, $\\mathbf{u}+\\mathbf{v}\\in L. \\quad {\\color{green}\\checkmark}$\n\n- Let's pick an arbitrary vector in $L,$ namely, ![](images/7f/7f7df4272eae1975b5802d54dfae499c528661ba0ae82faff45bb26f6cfab276.svg) Then, for any scalar $c,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}c\\cdot u & =c\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}4{u}_{1} \\\\ {u}_{1} \\\\ -2{u}_{1}\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}4\\cdot c{u}_{1} \\\\ c{u}_{1} \\\\ (-2)\\cdot c{u}_{1}\\end{aligned}],\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich satisfies the condition $\\dfrac x4=y=\\dfrac z{-2}.$ Therefore, $c\\cdot \\mathbf{u}\\in L. \\quad {\\color{green}\\checkmark}$\n\nThe set $L$ satisfies both conditions. So, we can conclude that it is a subspace of $\\mathbb{R}^3.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186121127 #edb/ref 1065 6859 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186121127 #edb/ref 1066 #uuid "fb0bb214-7f56-557a-82f7-baeef815457e" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186121127 #edb/ref 1067 "Lines as Subspaces of Three-Dimensional Space" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186121128 #edb/ref 1064 "Again, recall that $H$ is a subspace of $\\mathbb{R}^n$ if and only if it satisfies the following properties:\n\n- *Closure under vector addition:* \n\n$\\qquad$ If $\\mathbf{u}$ and $\\mathbf{v}$ are in $H,$ then their sum $\\mathbf{u}+\\mathbf{v}$ is also in $H.$\n\n- *Closure under scalar multiplication:* \n\n$\\qquad$ If $\\mathbf{u}$ is in $H,$ then $c\\cdot \\mathbf{u}$ is also in $H$ for any scalar number $c.$\n\nConsider the plane $\\Pi$ with equation $z=4x-2y$ in $\\mathbb{R}^3.$ \n\nNotice that $\\Pi$ contains vectors of the form \n\n![](images/95/956757aa0ce5b4721db582cd9d03dffaa21b1fdf6f8c60b5c6465f9cf012201d.svg)\n\nIs $\\Pi$ a subspace of $\\mathbb{R}^3?$ To find out, we need to check the two conditions above. \n\n- Let's pick two arbitrary vectors in $\\Pi,$ namely, ![](images/da/da46ac38012b220c5cf956076a81b76a21eb1242d21fae69f071531401d5d41d.svg) and ![](images/3e/3ee33188044335cd84f42b7621a5d255fdab972d89bef3d816c2b5a4da93dab0.svg) If we compute their sum, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u+v & =[\\begin{aligned}{u}_{1} \\\\ {u}_{2} \\\\ 4{u}_{1}-2{u}_{2}\\end{aligned}]+[\\begin{aligned}{v}_{1} \\\\ {v}_{2} \\\\ 4{v}_{1}-2{v}_{2}\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}{u}_{1}+{v}_{1} \\\\ {u}_{2}+{v}_{2} \\\\ 4{u}_{1}-2{u}_{2}+4{v}_{1}-2{v}_{2}\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}{u}_{1}+{v}_{1} \\\\ {u}_{2}+{v}_{2} \\\\ 4\\cdot ({u}_{1}+{v}_{1})-2\\cdot ({u}_{2}+{v}_{2})\\end{aligned}],\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich satisfies the condition $z=4x-2y.$ Therefore, $\\mathbf{u}+\\mathbf{v}\\in \\Pi. \\quad {\\color{green}\\checkmark}$\n\n- Let's pick an arbitrary vector in $\\Pi,$ say, ![](images/27/277ea18a8e1721fdaa880025e3abb4fcb1ff3e8ec96730ca7597fbd584ef6500.svg) Then, for any scalar $c$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}c\\cdot u & =c\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}{u}_{1} \\\\ {u}_{2} \\\\ 4{u}_{1}-2{u}_{2}\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}c{u}_{1} \\\\ c{u}_{2} \\\\ 4\\cdot c{u}_{1}-2\\cdot c{u}_{2}\\end{aligned}],\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich satisfies the condition $z=4x-2y.$ Therefore, $c\\cdot \\mathbf{u}\\in \\Pi. \\quad {\\color{green}\\checkmark}$\n\nThe set $\\Pi$ satisfies both conditions. So, we can conclude that it is a subspace of $\\mathbb{R}^3.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186121128 #edb/ref 1065 6860 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186121128 #edb/ref 1066 #uuid "3fcbfc6c-e5f6-5344-8ddd-779b625a03ea" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186121128 #edb/ref 1067 "Planes as Subspace of Three-Dimensional Space" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186121946 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186491621 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186121946 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186491655 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186121946 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186491726 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186121946 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186491803 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186121946 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186491824 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186121946 #edb/ref 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045493 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186121946 #edb/ref 1077 "selection" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186121946 #edb/ref 1078 #edb/ref 17592186491803 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186121946 #edb/ref 1079 #uuid "9fe8f42e-bd52-5557-a69d-1be3b48f9022" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186124504 #edb/ref 1037 #edb/ref 17592186048350 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186124504 #edb/ref 1037 #edb/ref 17592186049336 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186124504 #edb/ref 1037 #edb/ref 17592186050991 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186124504 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186124896 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186124504 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186124931 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186124504 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186125218 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186124504 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/r