86162847 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186162214 #edb/ref 1049 #edb/ref 17592186050021 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186162214 #edb/ref 1050 420.0 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186162214 #edb/ref 1050 420.0 #edb/ref 17592186045436] [17592186162214 #edb/ref 1053 #uuid "f83b8c51-727f-5eb0-9f6f-bcc3b133cfd6" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186162847 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186162859 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186162847 #edb/ref 1061 30531 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186162847 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "9c19c64f-0597-5273-8ff3-50729d7a67fc" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186162847 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186163222 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186162859 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186162924 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186162859 #edb/ref 1061 30550 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186162859 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "f2c61bc7-19cc-5e6a-a533-0450e49aa5dd" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186162859 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186161979 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186162924 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186162925 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186162924 #edb/ref 1061 31545 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186162924 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "a119eca9-e695-53d9-9049-eef3212a6e22" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186162924 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186163254 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186162925 #edb/ref 1061 31546 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186162925 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "dd82fa90-8f6a-5c31-a6bd-faa30ff2ee22" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186162925 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186162024 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186163156 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186163159 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186163156 #edb/ref 1061 44847 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186163156 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "b566ab50-5b2d-5693-aaa2-b74aeb985d53" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186163156 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186163365 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186163157 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186163158 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186163157 #edb/ref 1061 44848 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186163157 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "85e8d07f-ff5e-5a08-b15e-b9682157622a" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186163157 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186162153 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186163158 #edb/ref 1061 44851 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186163158 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "3bf9c7bd-4281-5c38-aa27-271d95010465" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186163158 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186162154 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186163159 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186163157 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186163159 #edb/ref 1061 44854 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186163159 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "b0b7073a-7020-52ef-b9e8-f169d03cd0f4" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186163159 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186162155 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186163222 #edb/ref 1064 "A square matrix $A$ is **symmetric** if it is equal to its transpose:\n\n$$\nA^T = A\n$$\n\nEquivalently, an $n\\times n$ matrix is symmetric if $a_{ij} = a_{ji}$ for all $1 \\leq i,j\\leq n.$\n\nIf $A$ is symmetric, this really means that it is symmetric across its main diagonal. \n\nFor example, the matrix \n\n![](images/0f/0f62a89f8da1ab7bfb86d46fddb441e95e5e3a9554e5a88662691b4df40e19af.svg)\n\nis symmetric since $A^T = A.$ \n\nOn the other hand, the matrix \n\n![](images/78/780de3c3f66b1037e4152224f4aa98b65b8b1b859adfe7c3881bb830d7eecd66.svg)\n\nis not symmetric. Indeed, since $b_{12} \\neq b_{21},$ we have $B^T \\neq B.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186163222 #edb/ref 1065 4880 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186163222 #edb/ref 1066 #uuid "1fe9d8bc-6133-5ab4-8b4f-af2b40817e02" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186163222 #edb/ref 1067 "Introduction" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186163254 #edb/ref 1064 "Suppose that $A$ is a symmetric matrix. Then, we have the following properties:\n\n- $-A$ is symmetric\n\n- $A^m$ is symmetric for any positive integer $m$\n\n- $A^{-1}$ is symmetric for nonsingular $A$\n\nIf $A$ and $B$ are $n\\times n$ symmetric matrices, we have the following properties:\n\n- $A+B$ is symmetric\n\n- The product $A\\cdot B$ is *not* always symmetric. For example, if \n![](images/e4/e41d28b6c6562ada530024fe6ab29cc16447dce14b2f741b8b35758618c78103.svg)\nthen the product $A\\cdot B$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A\\cdot B & =[\\begin{aligned}0 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 0\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 0\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 1\\end{aligned}]\\,\\,\\times \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \nwhich is *not* symmetric.\n\nFinally, the following two properties can be used to construct symmetric matrices:\n\n- $A + A^T$ is symmetric for any *square* matrix $A$\n\n- $A^T A$ and $A A^T$ are symmetric for *any* matrix $A$\n\nThe last property is true for *any* $m\\times n$ matrix. This property is particularly useful, as we'll discover in future lessons." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186163254 #edb/ref 1065 5043 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186163254 #edb/ref 1066 #uuid "c20572b7-1e75-5be2-a39c-b2327caff234" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186163254 #edb/ref 1067 "Properties of Symmetric Matrices" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186163365 #edb/ref 1064 "Let $X$ and $Y$ be discrete random variables with supports $S_X$ and $S_Y,$ respectively. We say that $X$ and $Y$ are **independent** if, for all $x\\in S_X$ and $y\\in S_Y,$ we have \n\n$$\nP(X=x, Y=y)=P(X=x)\\cdot P(Y=y).\n$$\n\nMoreover, if $X$ and $Y$ have the joint probability mass function $f(x,y),$ and $f_X(x)$ and $f_Y(y)$ are the marginal mass functions of $X$ and $Y,$ respectively, then\n\n$$\nf(x, y) = f_X(x)\\cdot f_Y (y)\n$$\n\n \n\nfor all $x\\in S_X$ and $y\\in S_Y.$\n\nIf the random variables are not independent, they are **dependent**." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186163365 #edb/ref 1065 7746 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186163365 #edb/ref 1066 #uuid "ab3a2705-ec4d-5e72-b6dd-553e9cbb6884" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186163365 #edb/ref 1067 "Introduction" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186172083 #edb/ref 1037 #edb/ref 17592186050412 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186172083 #edb/ref 103