75" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186172088 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186172282 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186172088 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186172284 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186172088 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186256686 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186172088 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186256689 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186172088 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186256690 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186172088 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186259551 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186172088 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186259552 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186172088 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186259553 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186172088 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186371665 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186172088 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186375804 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186172088 #edb/ref 1039 "Identifying a Three-Dimensional Vector Field" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186172088 #edb/ref 1041 #edb/ref 17592186171894 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186172088 #edb/ref 1042 #uuid "888aa30d-7179-507c-a30c-38209587333b" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186172171 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186172983 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186172171 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186172984 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186172171 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186172985 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186172171 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186172989 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186172171 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186172990 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186172171 #edb/ref 1044 #edb/ref 17592186045463 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186172171 #edb/ref 1045 "Visualizing Vector Fields" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186172171 #edb/ref 1046 #edb/ref 17592186172985 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186172171 #edb/ref 1049 #edb/ref 17592186048917 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186172171 #edb/ref 1050 420.0 #edb/ref 17592186045436] [17592186172171 #edb/ref 1053 #uuid "1bda195a-6aa9-5403-8c13-a26311503b1d" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186172983 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186172990 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186172983 #edb/ref 1061 35366 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186172983 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "8de1d301-7c80-5374-bd47-c7e3b3821626" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186172983 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186172083 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186172984 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186172989 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186172984 #edb/ref 1061 35367 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186172984 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "7533639d-bfe8-58f0-b64f-5c767246da78" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186172984 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186172084 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186172985 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186172983 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186172985 #edb/ref 1061 35369 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186172985 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "f845bde7-8e1b-5e89-8f3e-66a8c7e912eb" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186172985 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186173197 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186172989 #edb/ref 1061 35450 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186172989 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "64cb83b5-85c0-5f79-8253-5fd52adedcd8" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186172989 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186172088 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186172990 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186172984 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186172990 #edb/ref 1061 35467 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186172990 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "51e7b312-b65a-5a9c-88d2-2bb1e9411b96" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186172990 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186173198 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186173197 #edb/ref 1064 "A vector field on $\\mathbb R^2$ assigns a vector to every point $(x,y)$ in a region $S\\subset \\mathbb R^2$. We can visualize this by plotting the corresponding vector at each point in the coordinate plane.\n\nAs an example, let's sketch the vector field $\\mathbf F$ at the points $(1,1), (-3,3),$ and $(4,-3),$ where $\\mathbf F$ is given by\n\n$$\\mathbf F(x,y) = x \\, \\mathbf{i} + y \\, \\mathbf{j}.$$\n\nTo sketch the vector field at the given points, we first substitute their coordinates into the function $\\mathbf F\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\\begin{aligned}F(1,1) & =i+j \\\\ F(-3,3) & =-3\\,i+3\\,j \\\\ F(4,-3) & =4\\,i-3\\,j\\end{aligned}$$\n\nWe now draw our vectors, placing the tail ends of each vector at its corresponding point. This gives the following diagram:\n\n![](images/da/daea999f1a9bff587f0550e6674a39ae27dc9a2bb58e35877ed15626de0d9791.png)\n\nApplying the same method to more points in $\\mathbb{R}^2,$ we obtain the following visualization of our vector field:\n\n![](images/03/03402a7a7e1967b428c3657d6be90e4b1fd7ddd3c8ccaa11a69b0ff7cd566859.png)" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186173197 #edb/ref 1065 5763 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186173197 #edb/ref 1066 #uuid "6ed5e3d1-f4e2-5773-bbd0-5ef02d8b726d" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186173197 #edb/ref 1067 "Introduction" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186173198 #edb/ref 1064 "Given a visualization, it's possible to determine characteristics about the underlying vector field.\n\nFor example, let's consider the visualization below.\n\n![](images/6e/6e3ecb8b2d64aef1cf4654fea8e3ad8f176e9d830ee8b402a281155c3b4ef645.png)\n\nThis visualization was generated by one of the following functions.\n\n1. $\\mathbf F(x,y) = \\mathbf{i} - \\mathbf{j}$\n2. $\\mathbf G(x,y) = x \\, \\mathbf{i} + \\mathbf{j}$\n3. $\\mathbf H(x,y) = y \\, \\mathbf{i} + \\mathbf{j}$\n\nWe can determine which function generated the above diagram using a process of elimination:\n\n- The vertical component (i.e., the $\\mathbf j$ component) is positive for *every* vector in our vector field. This means we can exclude any function whose $\\mathbf j$ component is negative for some $(x,y) \\in \\mathbb{R}^2.$ Therefore, we can rule out the following option: $$\\mathbf F(x,y) = \\mathbf{i} - \\mathbf{j}$$\n- As we move away from the origin in the horizontal direction, our vectors' horizontal components (i.e., the $\\mathbf i$ components) get longer. This means that the $\\mathbf i$ component of our vector field is dependent on the variable $x.$\n\nSo, we can exclude any function whose $\\mathbf i$ component is independent of $x.$ Hence, we can rule out the following option: $$\\mathbf H(x,y) = y\\, \\mathbf{i} + \\mathbf{j}$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that the diagram corresponds to the vector field $\\mathbf G(x,y) = x \\, \\mathbf{i} + \\mathbf{j}.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186173198 #edb/ref 1065 5773 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186173198 #edb/ref 1066 #uuid "372dc560-67d1-5888-9353-8f8f365cff15" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186173198 #edb/ref 1067 "Analyzing Vector Field Diagrams" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186253755 #edb/ref 1080 "G(x,y,z)=-i" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186253755 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186253755 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "0af393b5-0b0a-51ee-963e-9232b624b8d6" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186253858 #edb/ref 1080 "H(x,y)=y\\,i+j" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186253858 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186253858 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "1594a1bc-f5fe-5f70-9851-0a40cf220726" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186254047 #edb/ref 1080 "F(x,y)=x\\,i" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186254047 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186254047 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "2a58e0ad-829d-534a-8952-ebde58ad8036" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186254058 #edb/ref 1080 "K(x,y)=-i-2\\,j" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186254058 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186254058 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "2ba98b8c-2116-50e2-b2de-6aae7dbae22c" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186254352 #edb/ref 1080 "K(x,y,z)={e}^{x}\\,i" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186254352 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186254352 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "4ebe45a6-6f14-5783-a2c5-0fff74a623ff" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186254460 #edb/ref 1080 "H(x,y,z)=i+j+k" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186254460 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498